multiple-choice questions for https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B

    llm.decide(state, "did the deployment succeed?", ["yes", "no", "unclear"])
    -> {"yes": 0.91, "no": 0.03, "unclear": 0.06}


how a language model answers

    a forward pass takes text in and puts out a score for every
    token in the vocabulary: how likely is each one to come next.
    to write an answer the model picks one token from those
    scores, appends it, and runs another forward pass. one pass
    per token, until it stops. then you read the text.


the trick

    ask "did the deployment succeed? A yes, B no, C unclear" and
    run one forward pass. the scores for A, B and C are already
    in the output. that is the decision. everything the model
    would write after this point is just spelling it out.

    so read the three scores, turn them into percentages, stop.


versus chat with a json grammar

    a grammar constrains generation: at every step, illegal
    tokens are masked, then one token is sampled from the rest.
    the text always parses. but it is still generation.

                       grammar                   readout
                       -------                   -------
    forward passes     one per token, ~4         one
    picks a token      yes, sampled               no
    returns            "yes"                      {yes .91, no .03, unclear .06}
    same prompt twice  can differ                 identical

    the argmax of readout is what a temperature-0 grammar would
    return. readout also gives you the other options' odds, and a
    51/49 coin flip looks like a coin flip instead of a confident
    "yes".


many questions, one document

    the document is prefilled once. each question is a few dozen
    tokens on top of it, and all of them run as one batch. 21
    questions cost about as much as one.


when not to use it

    anything longer than one token: sentences, numbers, lists,
    objects. those need generation, use chat.

    anything that needs thinking. readout reads the answer at the
    first position, before the model can reason. it is the gut
    reaction. a 27B gut reaction is decent, but for hard calls
    let it think and then use a grammar.


https://github.com/TheoLeeCJ/openjev did this with a 4B model.

quick demo:

    $ ./run.sh uv run example.py
